 ## Operating characteristics
-NoPe-NeRF outperforms existing methods in terms of novel view rendering quality and pose estimation accuracy on the Temples and ScanNet datasets [S1]. On the LLFF dataset, it achieves PSNR indicators 1-2 points higher for 3, 6, and 9 input views, and nearly 1 point higher than ViP-NeRF for three input views [S1]. The method is suitable for scenes with challenging camera trajectories where precomputed poses are unavailable or inaccurate [S2]. It requires a pre-trained monocular depth estimation network, which adds computational overhead during the depth generation step [S2].
+- **Time Complexity:** Depends on the depth estimation network and NeRF rendering. Generally slower than calibrated NeRF due to joint optimization.
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing depth maps and point clouds for all frames. Not given in the sources.
+- **Performance:** Outperforms existing methods in novel view rendering quality and pose estimation accuracy on Temples and ScanNet datasets [S1]. PSNR indicators on LLFF dataset are 1-2 points higher for 3, 6, and 9 input views [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Handles challenging camera trajectories; does not require precomputed poses; improves geometry reconstruction [S1, S2].
+- **Limitations:** Relies on the accuracy of the monocular depth estimation network; may struggle with highly dynamic scenes.
+
 ## Background
-NoPe-NeRF was proposed by Bian et al. in 2023 [S2]. It addresses the limitations of previous methods like SC-NeRF, which struggled with large camera movements [S2]. The method builds on the idea of using monocular depth priors to guide NeRF optimization, but introduces undistorted depth maps and a point cloud loss to improve robustness [S2].
-## Sources+NoPe-NeRF was proposed by Bian et al. in 2023 [S2]. It addresses the limitations of previous methods like BARF and SC-NeRF, which struggle with large camera motions or require specific scene constraints [S2]. The method builds on the idea of using monocular depth priors to guide NeRF optimization, introducing undistorted depth maps and a point cloud loss to stabilize training [S1].
